<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Middle Congo</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1958 CE · capital Brazzaville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony north of the Congo River whose capital, Brazzaville, governed French Equatorial Africa and briefly hosted Free France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-congo">French Congo</a> was divided in 1903, the lands between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a> and the Congo and Ubangi rivers became the colony of Middle Congo, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>, the post founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza</a> on land ceded by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tio">Kingdom of Tio</a>. From 1910 Brazzaville was also the seat of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Concession companies and forced labour dominated the early economy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-ocean-railway">Congo-Ocean railway</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a>, built between 1921 and 1934, cost the lives of many thousands of conscripted workers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-matsoua">André Matsoua's</a> movement challenged colonial rule from the 1920s. In 1940 Brazzaville became the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free France</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">de Gaulle</a> opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville-conference">Brazzaville Conference</a> there in 1944.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Middle Congo became the autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a> in November 1958 under the priest-politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulbert-youlou">Fulbert Youlou</a>, and gained full independence on 15 August 1960, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> as its capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/middle-congo.jpg" alt="Middle Congo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brazzaville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fulbert Youlou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Kikongo, Lingala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Moyen-Congo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=middle-congo" aria-label="Middle Congo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5124 2215 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Middle Congo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5444 2471L5262 2418M5444 2471L5424 2593"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5262 2418h0M5424 2593h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5444 2471h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5444 2471h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=middle-congo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
